speechtext:
Perhaps I could give the business of the House for Friday, which had originally been agreed upon to be an opposition day. Since that agreement has been changed, Friday will be a government day. We will commence on Friday with The Canada Development Corporation, followed by the Prairie Grain Advance Payments Act and subsequently the Judges Act. This will be the schedule for Friday. Subject to a further meeting of
June 2, 1971

House leaders which will take place early next week, there have been consultations concerning an order in the following terms on which, I understand, there may be unanimous agreement of the House: that on Mondays, June 7 and 14, and Tuesdays, June 8 and 15 and Thursdays, June 10 and 17 of this year, the ordinary hours of sitting of the House shall be from 11 a.m. to 1 p.m.; 2 p.m. to 6 p.m. and 8 p.m. to 10 p.m., and that, when any such day is designated as an allotted day for the consideration of the business of supply under the provisions of Standing Order 58, the proceedings on that business shall be interrupted or disposed of no later than 6 p.m., in order that government business may be considered from 8 p.m. to 10 p.m on such day and that the daily adjournment proceedings shall not be set aside by virtue of the operation of this order;
that for the purposes of section (5) of Standing Order 58, the number of sitting days allotted to the consideration of business of supply in the period ending not later than June 30, 1971 shall be fixed at ten, provided that an additional three sitting days shall be added to the total number of days allotted for the consideration of the business of supply in the period ending not later than December 10, 1971;
and that the order of business and procedure set out in section (10) of Standing Order 58 shall apply to the tenth allotted day in the current period and to the eighth allotted day in the next ensuring period.
Business of the House
